[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 3, "caption": "Figure 1.  P3GPT features a novel architecture enabling efficient omics-data training and multimodal extensions.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic model architecture diagram for multimodal biological/OMICS data processing.\n\nVisible elements:\n- Left panel: “Model input” showing an instruction token sequence, OMICS input matrices, downregulated gene/protein-like entries, annotation metadata, and a species/context table. Labels include examples such as `IGF1R`, `CDK?`, `TCF?`, `PKA`, and annotations like Species: human, Age: 40, Sex: M, Tissue: liver, Health: T2D, and dataset identifiers.\n- Middle-left panel: “OMICS Data” with listed biological source modalities:\n  - Transcriptomics\n  - Epigenetics\n  - Proteomics\n- A green “Modality Mapper (MM)” block transforms OMICS data into a fixed embedding shape, shown as `[ n × k ] kG × Text embeddings`, using modules labeled `FF`, `B.norm`, `ReLU`, `FF`, producing `[ n × 256 ]`.\n- Lower middle inputs: separate “Text Data” and “Knowledge Graph Data” boxes, each passed through small green “MM” mapper blocks.\n- Center panel: “Learnable embeddings” and “Multi-modal Input”, both represented as colored token/embedding grids with dimension labels such as `×256`.\n- Right panel: stacked “Transformer Block” modules, each marked `×32`. One stack has a flame icon and note “Freeze weights”; another stack has a crossed/snowflake-like icon suggesting frozen or altered training behavior.\n- Top-right inset: transformer block internals labeled `MHA`, `Norm`, `FF`, `Norm`, and `TB`, with notes defining:\n  - ALiBi = Attention with Linear Biases\n  - MHA = Multi-Head Attention\n  - FF = Feed-Forward\n  - B.Norm = Batch Normalization\n- Top-right also shows ALiBi attention/bias matrices feeding into MHA.\n- Bottom-right “Novelty” section lists claimed contributions:\n  - Modalities first combined: multimodal OMICS, knowledge graph, and text\n  - New tokenization method for OMICS data\n  - Task solving in a language modelling way\n  - Unique multiomics multispecies dataset\n\nOverall, the figure depicts a multimodal transformer-based architecture that maps OMICS, text, and knowledge graph data into shared embeddings for language-model-style processing."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 4, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a workflow schematic for an AI-driven biomedical research system.\n\nVisible components and labels:\n- Left column: human roles in boxed panels:\n  - “Data curator”: extend DB with new data, fine-tune P3GPT, harmonize datasets.\n  - “Investigator”: simulates lab experiments, comes up with research hypotheses and tests.\n  - “Lab technician”: check inventory, reserve facilities, prepare treatment protocol.\n  - “Scholar”: distill experimental results, disseminate findings, identify research direction.\n- Center vertical pipeline:\n  - “Datasets” feeding into “Precious-3 GPT” with label “Trains the model.”\n  - “Precious-3 GPT” labeled as providing a hypothesis.\n  - “Robotics Lab” labeled as generating experimental data.\n  - “Science42: DORA” labeled as producing academic text.\n- Right column:\n  - “Database” panel listing domains: species, omics, tissues, cell lines, compounds, diseases, demographics.\n  - Omics: DNAm, expression, proteomics.\n  - Modalities: tabular omics, knowledge graph, text.\n  - Source logos include TCGA, PubMed, LINCS, ARCHS4, GEO, pandaOmics, CCLE, GTEx, UK Biobank, INDRA.\n  - “Research areas” panels:\n    - Drug Discovery: repurpose known drugs, model preclinical studies, look for mimetics.\n    - Natural Compound Modeling: screen safe compounds, compose dietary regimens.\n    - Omics Interpretation: model case-control results, annotate samples with any omics, get compound signatures.\n    - Geroprotector Discovery: effect of compounds on aging rate, MOA similarity to senolytics, preclinical model simulation.\n- Directional arrows:\n  - Green dashed arrows indicate data retrieval/integration, research-problem formalization, lab validation requests, data retrieval, pain-point finding, and outputs toward research areas.\n  - Gray dashed arrows show the central pipeline flow from datasets to model to robotics lab to Science42/DORA.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Biomedical databases and omics resources are represented, including genomics/omics, diseases, tissues, cell lines, compounds, demographics, DNAm, expression, and proteomics.\n- No microscopy images, molecular structures beyond small icons, or experimental plots are shown.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- Data are curated and harmonized into datasets/databases.\n- Datasets train the “Precious-3 GPT” model.\n- The model provides hypotheses.\n- A robotics lab generates experimental data.\n- Science42/DORA produces academic text.\n\nFindings:\n- No quantitative experimental results or specific biological findings are presented; the figure describes a proposed workflow/system architecture."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 11, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure labeled A-F.\n\nPanel A: Two bar charts showing age group distributions. Left: “Human age groups, N = 18,224,” x-axis age in years, y-axis counts in thousands. Right: “Mouse age groups, N = 23,024,” x-axis age in days, y-axis counts in thousands.\n\nPanel B: Bar chart of “Top-20 tissue counts (59.65% of all data),” showing sample counts by tissue type. Visible tissues include breast, prostate, lung, kidney, liver, colon, blood, uterus, pancreas, bladder, bone, mesothelium, endometrium, ovary, heart, leukocyte, nasopharynx, bone marrow, lymph, and astrocyte.\n\nPanel C: Line plot of model training/evaluation cross-entropy loss versus steps in thousands. Blue line is Training, orange line is Evaluation. Loss decreases rapidly early and then gradually plateaus.\n\nPanel D: Boxplots of Jaccard similarity for genes grouped as Upregulated and Downregulated. Models compared are P3GPT, GPT4o, and Random. P3GPT has visibly higher similarity than GPT4o and Random for both gene groups, with significance brackets marked by ***.\n\nPanel E: Two density/contour plots comparing predicted age versus actual age in years. Left plot is Horvathv1 with R² = 0.54 and N = 2,206. Right plot is P3GPT with R² = 0.90 and N = 2,824. Both include a dashed diagonal reference line; P3GPT shows tighter agreement with actual age.\n\nPanel F: Heatmap of Pearson correlations among epigenetic/aging predictors: Horvathv1, Hannum, Lin, PhenoAge, DunedinPACE, DNAmGrimAge, and P3GPT-based. Color scale ranges approximately from 0.2 to 1.0. P3GPT-based correlates strongly with Horvathv1 at 0.81 and more weakly with several others.\n\nBiological source objects visible: human and mouse age-group datasets, tissue samples, gene sets categorized as upregulated/downregulated, and aging/epigenetic clock predictors. The figure presents dataset composition, model training behavior, gene similarity evaluation, age prediction performance, and correlation among aging predictors."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 17, "caption": "Figure 4. P3GPT shows unique capabilities, such as age prediction in unseen species, discovery of novel geroprotectors, and cross-omics target discovery.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure with panels labeled A, B, and C.\n\nPanel A: Scatter plot comparing “Actual age, years/lifespan” on the x-axis with “Predicted age, years/lifespan” on the y-axis. Points are colored/shaped by species and tissue/source type. Visible species include beluga whale, bottlenose dolphin, cattle, cat, dog, horse, human, mouse, naked mole rat, pig, rat, rhesus macaque, roe deer, sheep, vervet, and yellow-bellied marmot. Tissue/source labels in the legend include blood, skin, brain, cerebellum, cerebral cortex, heart, kidney, liver, muscle, spleen, striatum, and ear. A pink “Overall Trendline” runs diagonally upward, indicating a positive relationship between actual normalized age and predicted normalized age.\n\nPanel B: Dose-response style line plot titled “XL-888.” The y-axis is “N total cells (relative to DMSO)” and the x-axis is “Log10(M),” with a separated DMSO control region and drug concentrations labeled 20 nM, 100 nM, and 1 uM. Two conditions are shown: siNC in blue and siβPIX in orange. Both decrease with increasing XL-888 concentration, with siβPIX lower than siNC at tested doses.\n\nPanel C: Three-set Venn diagram comparing “Proteome,” “Transcriptome,” and “Methylation,” plus a separate circle labeled “None.” Overlap counts are shown: None = 9, Proteome only = 2, Methylation only = 4, Transcriptome-only appears 0, shared Proteome/Transcriptome = 2, shared all three = 7, and several other pairwise/region counts are 0.\n\nOverall, the figure combines model prediction performance across species/tissues, a drug perturbation cell-count assay, and multi-omics overlap analysis."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 19, "caption": "Figure 5. P3GPT uses a rigid prompt structure to formalize research settings and communicate with the user.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific schematic with panels labeled **A** and **B**.\n\nPanel **A** shows a prompt-like structured text input using XML-style tags. Visible fields include:\n- `<drug> UNKNOWN </drug>`\n- `<species> m. musculus </species>`\n- `<tissue> muscle </tissue>`\n- `<datatype> expression </datatype>`\n- `<up> ORM2 DEFB4B </up>`\n- `<down> MUC5AC TBC1D3E MSMB </down>`\n- `<sex> UNKNOWN </sex>`\n- `<age> UNKNOWN </age>`\n\nA green-highlighted instruction tag reads `<instr> diff2cpd </instr>`. A downward arrow points to predicted drug outputs:\n- metformin\n- semaglutide\n- acarbose\n\nPanel **B** depicts the **Precious3GPT** model workflow. On the left, an input card contains an age comparison:\n- control age group: 20-30 years\n- case age group: 70-80 years\n\nThis is paired with an orange instruction block labeled approximately `<age2diff> instruction`. The model output is shown as:\n- UP-regulated genes: A, B, C\n- DOWN-regulated genes: X, Y, Z\n\nThese outputs are transformed or crossed into another **Precious3GPT** card on the right, where the gene regulation direction is reversed:\n- UP-regulated genes: X, Y, Z\n- DOWN-regulated genes: A, B, C\n\nThis second card uses an orange instruction block labeled approximately `<diff2compound> instruction`, producing a final model output: **“List of potential geroprotectors.”**\n\nBiological source objects shown include mouse muscle gene expression features and gene-regulation signatures. The figure illustrates transformations from biological metadata and differential gene-expression signatures to candidate compounds/geroprotectors using Precious3GPT instruction-based model interfaces."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 35, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic model architecture for a multimodal biomedical/omics transformer.\n\nVisible elements:\n- A legend defines abbreviations: `Alibi` as Attention with Linear Biases, `MHA` as Multi-Head Attention, `FF` as Feed-Forward, and `B.Norm` as Batch Normalization.\n- Left panel labeled `Model input` shows structured input tokens:\n  - `Instruction`: example text `cpd-2-diff`.\n  - `OMICs`: upregulated and downregulated feature matrices with example labels `IGFR1`, `COX2`, `TGFB`, `PKA`.\n  - `Annotation`: metadata examples including species human, age 40, sex M, tissue liver, health T2D, compound NMN, and data RNAseq.\n  - Embedding width indicated as `×256`.\n- Middle-left data sources:\n  - `OMICs Data` with listed modalities: transcriptomics, epigenetics, proteomics.\n  - `Text Data`.\n  - `Knowledge Graph Data`.\n- A green `Modality Mapper (MM)` block maps `[n × k] KG or text embeddings` through layers labeled `FF`, `B.Norm`, `ReLU`, `FF` into `[n × 256]`.\n- Center panel shows:\n  - `Learnable embeddings` represented as stacked colored token grids, labeled `×256`.\n  - `Multi-modal input`, combining the colored omics/annotation token blocks with additional green mapped tokens; several snowflake and flame icons indicate frozen or trainable components.\n- Right side model interface:\n  - Stacked `Transformer Block` modules, each marked `×32`.\n  - Upper transformer path appears trainable, indicated with flame icons.\n  - Lower transformer path is labeled `Freeze weights`, with a downward arrow and snowflake icons indicating frozen weights.\n  - An `ALiBi` module is shown feeding into attention, with matrices labeled `Attn.` and `Biases`, multiplied by `m`.\n  - A detailed `TB` block shows repeated sequence: `MHA`, `Norm`, `FF`, `Norm`, with residual-style connections, labeled `×32`.\n- Bottom-right `Novelty` claims:\n  - Modalities first combined: OMICs multimodal, knowledge graph, and text.\n  - New tokenization method for OMICs data.\n  - Task solving in a language modeling way.\n  - Unique multiomics multispecies dataset.\n\nBiological source objects visible:\n- Omics measurements and annotations.\n- Example genes/proteins/pathway-like labels: `IGFR1`, `COX2`, `TGFB`, `PKA`.\n- Data types include transcriptomics, epigenetics, proteomics, RNAseq, text data, and knowledge graph data."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 37, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nPanel A is visible. It shows a histogram/bar chart titled “Human age groups” with `N = 18,224`. The x-axis is labeled “Age, years” with binned age groups from `0-10` through `>100`, and the y-axis is labeled “Counts, K”. Light-blue vertical bars show the distribution of human samples/individuals across age bins, with the highest counts around middle-to-late adult ages, especially roughly `50-80`.\n\nA partial panel label `B` is visible at the bottom edge, but its content is cropped out.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, molecular structures, transformations, or model interfaces are visible in this extracted image."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 37, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nA single plot panel labeled “C” shows model training performance over steps.\n\nVisible elements:\n- X-axis: “Steps, k”\n- Y-axis: “Cross-entropy loss”\n- Legend title: “Cohort”\n- Blue line: “Training”\n- Orange line: “Evaluation”\n- Both curves decrease steeply at early steps and then gradually flatten.\n- The training curve starts higher, around 7.5, and approaches about 6.2.\n- The evaluation curve starts near 7.0 and also approaches about 6.2.\n- The two curves converge closely by later steps, suggesting similar training and evaluation loss behavior.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, molecular structures, anatomical objects, transformations, or model architecture/interface diagrams are visible in this cropped image."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 37, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nPanel labeled **D**. The figure shows two grouped box-and-scatter plots of **Jaccard similarity** on the y-axis, comparing gene-set overlap for **Upregulated** and **Downregulated** genes on the x-axis.\n\nVisible categories/models in the legend:\n- **P3GPT**: light purple\n- **GPT4o**: coral/red\n- **Random**: teal/green\n\nEach gene category contains three boxplots with overlaid points. Statistical significance brackets are shown above comparisons, with labels such as `***`.\n\nMain visible finding: **P3GPT has higher Jaccard similarity than GPT4o and Random** for both upregulated and downregulated genes, while Random is lowest. No biological source organism, cell type, experimental assay, or model interface details are visible in this cropped image."]}, {"figure_index": 10, "page_no": 37, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nExtracted panel labeled “E” showing two side-by-side density/scatter comparison plots of predicted age versus actual age.\n\nVisible panels:\n- Left panel: “Horvathv1”\n  - Text: “R²=0.54”, “N = 2,206”\n  - X-axis: “Actual age, years”\n  - Y-axis: “Predicted age, years”\n  - Includes a dashed diagonal reference line.\n  - Colored density/count contours over a blue background, with a vertical “Counts” color scale at right.\n\n- Right panel: “P3GPT”\n  - Text: “R²=0.90”, “N = 2,824”\n  - X-axis: “Actual age, years”\n  - Y-axis shared/implicit as predicted age.\n  - Includes a dashed diagonal reference line.\n  - Colored density/count contours over a blue background, with a vertical “Counts” color scale at right.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- The cropped figure itself does not visibly identify the biological sample type, organism, assay, or tissue.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- Two age prediction models or methods are compared: Horvathv1 and P3GPT.\n- Output appears to be predicted biological/epigenetic age plotted against actual chronological age.\n\nFindings visible:\n- P3GPT shows stronger agreement with actual age than Horvathv1, with higher reported R²: 0.90 versus 0.54.\n- P3GPT points/density align more closely with the diagonal reference line."]}, {"figure_index": 11, "page_no": 37, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nA single heatmap panel showing a Pearson’s correlation matrix, with a color bar labeled “Pearson’s r” ranging roughly from 0.2 to 1.0. Rows and columns list methods/datasets: Horvathv1, Hannum, Lin, PhenoAge, DunedinPACE, DNAmGrimAge, and P3GPT-based.\n\nEach cell contains a numeric correlation value. The diagonal is 1.00. Strong correlations appear among several established epigenetic age measures, for example Hannum–Lin 0.87, Lin–PhenoAge 0.97, PhenoAge–DNAmGrimAge 0.94, and Hannum–DNAmGrimAge 0.94. P3GPT-based shows high correlation with Horvathv1 at 0.81, but lower correlations with other methods, around 0.19 to 0.38.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy, tissue images, molecular structures, or model interface diagrams are visible. The figure is a statistical comparison heatmap of epigenetic age-related predictors or clocks."]}, {"figure_index": 12, "page_no": 39, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a schematic/model overview with two visible panels.\n\nLeft panel: A boxed interface labeled “Precious3GPT” takes an “Age group” input and an “Age group label,” shown with small code/bracket icons, and feeds into a yellow module labeled as a “regulable … transformer” or similar transformer component. The output connects toward a middle “Model output” section.\n\nMiddle/right panel: A second “Precious3GPT” boxed workflow shows “UP-regulated genes: gene A, B, C” and “DOWN-regulated genes: genes X, Y, Z” as model outputs. These gene lists are passed into a larger orange module labeled as a transformer/interface component, producing or associating with another set of predicted regulated genes: “UP-regulated genes: X, Y, Z” and “DOWN-regulated genes: A, B, C.”\n\nVisible biological source objects: gene sets/lists, specifically up-regulated and down-regulated genes.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces: age group metadata appears to be converted into regulation-related gene outputs using a GPT/transformer-style model interface; gene regulation lists are then mapped or transformed into another predicted gene-regulation output.\n\nFindings shown: the figure depicts a model pipeline for predicting or transforming up- and down-regulated gene sets, but no quantitative results, organisms, tissues, datasets, or validation metrics are visible."]}, {"figure_index": 13, "page_no": 39, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["NON_SCIENTIFIC_IMAGE\n\nThe image appears to be a small UI or slide snippet labeled “Model output,” showing a green document-like icon and text that reads approximately “List of potential geroprotectors.” It does not contain scientific figure panels, experimental data, biological imagery, plots, microscopy, model architecture, or labeled transformations."]}]

